I added a test file, tests/print_stdout_contract.rs, that runs the real yoyo binary against a fake model server and checks that -p writes the answer to stdout exactly once. It guards #966, and nothing in src/ changed. Task 1 had already landed (054c2dfc), so none of the tests needed to be #[ignore]d.

The fake server: it listens on 127.0.0.1:0 and replies with a short Anthropic streaming response whose only text is {"answer": 42}. Each run uses a temporary home directory and working directory, so no real user config or trust store is read. It uses a dummy API key and is killed after 20 seconds. yoyo accepted the stream on the first try, with no changes needed.

The four tests, all checking stdout bytes:
• **--print:** stdout is exactly the answer (assert_eq!) and contains it only once.
• **`--output-format json`:** stdout parses as exactly one JSON value, and that value contains the answer.
• **Stdin, no -p, with --print:** the answer appears exactly once.
• **Near-miss:** plain -p without --print still streams the answer to stdout.

Every test also checks that the fake server actually received a request, so a run that never reached the network can't pass on empty output.

Positive control: I ran one command that swapped src/ back to the code before the #966 fix, ran the tests, and restored src/. Three tests failed by name:
• `print_mode_emits_the_answer_exactly_once`
• `json_mode_emits_exactly_one_value`
• `piped_print_mode_emits_the_answer_exactly_once`

The near-miss test stayed green. In json mode the old bug didn't break parsing: the leaked answer is valid JSON on its own, so stdout parsed as two values. That's why the test counts values instead of just checking that the output parses. The tree was clean after the restore, and nothing marked NEUTERED is left.

cargo fmt, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, and the full cargo test suite pass.

There are two commits. f5f11062 holds the test file itself, committed as soon as the fake server worked. 56141e5a is the titled task commit; it's empty because the file was already committed, and it records the control result in its message. I didn't update any docs because nothing user-facing changed.
